lletin for August 1982, referencing \"as of reported values on the end of the 1982 FY\". The data for August 1981 is clearly 13.61, but for August 1982 we only have weekly data available. I'll calculate the average of those weekly values: 13.42, 13.25, 12.98, 12.69, and 12.87.\n\nI realize there might be more nuance in interpreting the yield values. Some entries have footnote markers like \"4/\" or \"5/\" that could indicate specific calculation methods or adjustments. For August 1981 in the 1981 column, the value is simply 13.61 without any special notation, suggesting it's a straightforward value.\n\nThe footnote explains that \"4/\" means one or more new long-term bonds were added to the average, while \"5/\" indicates an existing bond was dropped from the long-term bond average. These annotations could potentially impact the yield calculation.\n\nI'll focus on calculating the average and symmetric growth rate, considering the August 1982 weekly data provides additional context. The footnote markers don't appear to significantly alter the interpretation of the yields for my current calculations.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_1271e0918af24e8aa006f628","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me reconsider the problem with more precision\n# Looking at the question: \"as of reported values on the end of the 1982 FY\"\n# This means values as they appear in the document at end of FY 1982\n\n# From the table, we have:\n# August 1981 (1981 column): 13.61\n# August 1982 (1982 column): empty - no value\n\n# But there's weekly data in the second table showing August 1982 weeks\n# with their yields. These would be the \"as reported\" values at that time.\n\n# For August 1982, the weekly data shows:\naug_2 = 13.42\naug_9 = 13.25\naug_16 = 12.98\naug_23 = 12.69\naug_30 = 12.87\n\naug_1982 = (aug_2 + aug_9 + aug_16 + aug_23 + aug_30) / 5\naug_1981 = 13.61\n\nprint(f\"August 1981 Treasury yield: