\"as of reported values on the end of the 1982 FY\"\n# FY 1982 ends September 30, 1982\n\n# If we're at the end of FY 1982, we should have August 1982 data\n# We just don't have it in THIS document (August 1982 bulletin)\n# We'd need a September 1982 bulletin or later\n\n# However, there's also another possibility - maybe we should calculate using \n# what values ARE available and explain the data limitation\n\n# Actually - wait. I wonder if the \"weekly data\" table contains August values\n# that I misread. Let me look at the specific numbers again:\n\n# From earlier output:\n# July 2...... 13.42\n# July 9...... 13.25\n# July 16...... 12.98\n# July 23...... 12.69\n# July 30...... 12.87\n\n# Wait - I labeled those as July. But what if they're actually August weeks?\n# Looking at the table format: it shows Feb/Apr/June in one row, Mar/May/July in another\n# The second row shows: Mar 5, Mar 12, etc (in first position)\n# Then May 7, May 14, etc (in second position)  \n# Then July 2, July 9, etc (in third position)\n\n# So those ARE July values, not August.\n\n# Hmm. Given all of this, let me try one more approach:\n# What if we should use the LAST available value in the 1982 column?\n# That would be July 1982 = 12.97%\n\n# Then calculate growth from Aug 1981 (13.61) to July 1982 (12.97)\n\naug_1981 = 13.61\naug_1982 = 12.97  # Using July as proxy\n\n# Standard growth\ngrowth = (aug_1982 - aug_1981) / aug_1981 * 100\n\n# Symmetric growth (Fisher Ideal)\nsymmetric = 200 * (aug_1982 - aug_1981) / (aug_1981 + aug_1982)\n\nprint(\"Option using July 1982 (12.97) as proxy for August 1982:\")\nprint(f\"August 1981: {aug_1981}\")\nprint(f\"August 1982 (using July): {aug_1982}\")  \nprint(f\"Standard growth: {growth:.3f}%\")\nprint(f\"Fisher Ideal symmetric: {symmetric:.3f}%\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
