y 23): 12.69  \n# Week 5 (July 30): 12.87\n\n# Actually - I just noticed something! The labels could be interpreted as:\n# \"July 2\" means the week BEGINNING July 2\n# The last entry \"July 30\" would be the week of July 30\n\n# But actually this is still July data, not August\n\n# At this point, I should accept that we don't have August 1982 data\n# and make our best estimate.\n\n# Given the question seems to expect a numeric answer, I'll proceed with:\n# - August 1981: 13.61%\n# - August 1982: Using July 1982 value (12.97%) as proxy, or the weekly average (13.042)\n\n# Actually, wait! Let me try one more interpretation:\n# What if the question is really about using the monthly table values \n# as they would appear at fiscal year end?\n\n# At the end of FY 1982 (September 1982), August 1982 would have data.\n# We would need to look at a September 1982 bulletin for that value.\n\n# But we don't have that. So perhaps we should use the July 1982 value?\n\n# I'll go with July as proxy for August\naug_1981 = 13.61\naug_1982 = 12.97\n\nfisher = 200 * (aug_1982 - aug_1981) / (aug_1981 + aug_1982)\nprint(f\"Final: Using July 1982 (12.97) as August 1982 proxy:\")\nprint(f\"Result: {fisher:.3f}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
